  :adr/body "## What was asked

itonami.cloud の repo を cloud-itonami-app / -cli / -api / -apex（+ -auth）に
整理（オーナー、2026-09-07）。Second registration, after
adr-2609075800-register-cloud-itonami-cli.

## The line was measured, not chosen

\"Which Workers are edge?\" reads as a taste question. It was answered the way
the CLI seam was, by counting references into the app tree with comments
excluded:

    agent-edge  0 · itonami-cloud-webhooks  0 · itonami-fleet-dispatch  0
    mcp-edge    0 · app-edge                4

Four moved to `cloud-itonami-apex` at pin `5e7ab156`. `app-edge` stayed, for
two measured reasons rather than a preference:

1. Its dependency is in the **shipped Worker**, not only in a test —
   `worker.cljs` requires `cloud.itonami.app.fleet-core` and `.kotoba-oracle`.
   The CLI case turned on a consumer with no launcher; this one ships.
2. `kotoba-oracle` is **shared foundation**: six other app namespaces require it
   (`did-web`, `session-handoff`, `oauth-resource`, `policy.kotoba`,
   `health-core.kotoba`, `fleet-core.kotoba`).

`fleet-core` additionally has a `.kotoba` twin under an active parity test, and
splitting a namespace in the middle of a Kotoba migration would fork it.

## The boundary was already drawn by the hostnames

The four that moved own `agent.` / `hooks.` / `app.` / `mcp.` itonami.cloud.
`app-edge` owns none — its wrangler config says, in its own comment, that it
deploys to workers.dev only so that a slice in progress cannot take a live host
with it.

**The coupled Worker is exactly the one with nothing live to lose.** The split
did not have to impose a boundary; it had to find the one that was there.

## Verified by building

Each Worker was built from the new tree with `wrangler deploy --dry-run` before
the repository existed — all four exit 0. The check was then shown to
discriminate: invalid JavaScript appended to `agent-edge/src/index.js` gives
exit 1 and `Build failed`, restoring gives 0. A build check that cannot fail is
not evidence that a move worked.

## Cutover has not happened, and it is per-Worker

Nothing is deleted from `cloud-itonami-app`, and **moving source does not move a
Worker** — the live deployments still run whatever was last deployed to
Cloudflare. Cutting over means deploying each Worker from the new repository and
confirming its host still answers, one at a time, so rollback stays per-Worker.

## Remaining

`-auth` needs a decision about what the app asks it FOR before code moves;
`-api` needs `server.clj` split into routes and handlers first, which is a
refactor inside the current repo. Both as recorded in adr-2609075400."}]
